# DO Manager OHR Project Conventions

## Context
- This rule applies to all TypeScript and React files in the project
- File organization follows Next.js 15 App Router patterns
- Project uses React 19, TypeScript, and Tailwind CSS for styling

## Requirements
- Use React Server Components by default and add 'use client' directive only when necessary
- Maintain consistent directory structure and naming conventions
- Follow established component patterns and organization
- Use proper TypeScript interfaces and types

## File Structure and Organization
- Place client components in `src/components` directory
- Organize context providers in `src/context` directory
- Keep custom hooks in `src/hooks` directory
- Define TypeScript interfaces in `src/types` directory
- Organize utility functions in `src/utils` directory
- Place API services in `src/services` directory

## Component Naming and Styling
- Use PascalCase for component file names and export names
- Use kebab-case for directory names (e.g., `bucket-selector.tsx`)
- UI components must be in the `src/components/ui` directory
- Use named exports for components instead of default exports
- Feature components should be organized in their own directories

## Coding Standards
- Use TypeScript interfaces for component props
- Name interfaces with prefix of component name followed by "Props" (e.g., `BucketSelectorProps`)
- Use React Context API for global state management
- Export context hooks with "use" prefix (e.g., `useBucket`)
- Include proper error handling for context hooks (check for undefined)
- Use function declarations for components (not arrow functions)
- Use Tailwind CSS for styling with className strings

## React Patterns
- Use custom hooks for reusable logic
- Context providers should include loading states
- Use Next.js navigation hooks (`useRouter`, `usePathname`, `useSearchParams`)
- Always include TypeScript types for component props and state
- Implement proper error handling in async functions

